Verified task rule: a full line of color 6 separates rectangular regions; one small tile repeats across each adjacent region while the separator remains intact. The demos disambiguated the axis: in demo 1 a vertical separator at column 4 divided a 4-column tile from a 4-column region; the tile pattern replicated to the right. In demo 2 a horizontal separator at row 3 divided a 3-row tile from a 3-row region; the top tile replicated downward. The test combined both: a 3x6 tile at upper-left, a full vertical separator column at index 6 and full horizontal separator rows at indices 3 and 7. Replicate tile values across and down the non-separator regions; retain the separator lines as color 6. The resulting 11x13 submission was confirmed correct.

Generalizable check: determine tile dimensions as the distance from grid boundary to separator; tile each region using corresponding tile coordinates. Verify all dimensions and preserve separator cells. Don't infer replication from only one separator orientation when both dimensions or irregular region sizes could support alternative rules.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545321/attempts/695f0949c61e4d9285c5659d167e433e/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-evidence-processing/references/repeating-separator-tiles.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-synthesis", "file": "references/sparse-pattern-completion-lesson.md", "content": "# Selective sparse-pattern completion: unresolved row-selection rule

This session supplied eight heterogeneous demonstrations for a task whose test was a 29×24 grid with sparse 7-patterns on background 5. In several demonstrations, a few cells at the right ends of selected rows/regions changed from background to 1. Examples included right-edge row tails (2 or 4 cells, depending on grid width), a small 2×2 corner patch, and other selective edge blocks. Many visually similar rows and repeated-looking counterparts remained untouched. Full-width bands were often unchanged except for a single anomalous cell, which then became 1. The final pair reinforced that tail extension occurs only for selected patterns, not every non-background row.

Two test submissions were wrong: first the input was preserved, then alternating background cells in a row were recolored. Neither demonstrates a validated rule. The selection predicate remains unresolved. For future instances, classify this as sparse-pattern completion/repair, but do not fill all row tails, all repeated rows, or a broad rectangle. Transcribe all changed and unchanged cells from each pair, form competing explicit selection hypotheses, and reproduce every demo cellwise before testing. If the demo cap is reached without a rule, submit only the best-supported candidate and never label it validated.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545321/attempts/695f0949c61e4d9285c5659d167e433e/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-synthesis/references/sparse-pattern-completion-lesson.md"}

